```java
class Cache<K, V> {
    private final Map<K, V> map = new HashMap<>();
    private final ReadWriteLock lock = new ReentrantReadWriteLock();

    V get(K key) {
        lock.readLock().lock();
        try { return map.get(key); }
        finally { lock.readLock().unlock(); }
    }

    void put(K key, V value) {
        lock.writeLock().lock();
        try { map.put(key, value); }
        finally { lock.writeLock().unlock(); }
    }
}
```

This is Java's `ReentrantReadWriteLock`, but the underlying idea -- a **read-write lock** -- generalizes: any number of readers can hold the read lock simultaneously, since concurrent reads can't corrupt anything, but a writer needs exclusive access, blocking every reader and writer until it's done.

For a cache that's read far more often than written -- the common case -- this beats a plain single lock, where every read blocks every other read for no reason.
